  "Name": "Dobrowolski1970-filterA",
  "Source": "J. A. Dobrowolski, \"Optical Interference Filters for the Adjustment of Spectral Response and Spectral Power Distribution,\" Appl. Opt. 9, 1396-1402 (1970). doi:10.1364/AO.9.001396 - Table I (Filter A), p. 1400; Sec. III.B and Fig. 6, p. 1399",
  "Notes": "Two-layer component filter A of a constant-output-monochromator correction filter (15 filters A + 5 filters B in series, Sec. III.B)\nSpecification: The series system of 15 identical filters A and 5 identical filters B should have a transmittance proportional to the inverse of the measured spectral response of the monochromator + thermopile (dotted curve of Fig. 6), varying over almost three decades between 0.3 and 0.7 um; highest index allowed 1.90 (PbF2, transparent at 0.3 um). Only the relative shape mattered.\nReported: Only as a plot: Fig. 6 (log scale) gives the calculated transmittance of the 15A + 5B series system (full curve) versus the desired curve (dotted). Approximate readings from the plot (not printed numbers): ~50 % at 0.30-0.33 um, ~30 % at 0.35 um, ~3 % near 0.42 um, ~1 % near 0.50 um, minimum ~0.27 % near 0.62-0.63 um, rising to ~0.4 % at 0.70 um. To compare, recompute T_sys = T_A^15 * T_B^5 (incoherent series, ignoring multiple reflections between filters, as the paper evidently does).\nNotes: Incident medium air (Table I medium 1.000), substrate 1.450 (fused silica-like, as printed). Whether the substrate back surface is included is not stated. Table lists Substrate, layers 1..N, Medium; layer 1 is next to the substrate (the synthesis program grows the design from the bare substrate), so the order is REVERSED here (layer 1 below = last printed layer, next to the medium). Thicknesses are printed as optical thicknesses n*d in micrometres; they are entered as FWOT at reference_nm = 1000 nm, which is the same number (1 FWOT at 1000 nm = 1 um optical thickness), with constant, non-dispersive, lossless indices exactly as printed. Physical thickness d = OT/n. Normal incidence assumed (not stated). 2 layers, as stated in the text; summed optical thickness of the printed layers = 0.2660 um (no total is printed).\nLayers from Table I (Filter A), p. 1400; Sec. III.B and Fig. 6, p. 1399; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
